it may be small but the mini has a big  place in british automotive history the  car began rolling off the assembly lines  in birmingham in 1959 as the austin  seven and the morris mini miner just six  years later in 1965 designer alec  izagonis personally handed over the keys  to the owner of the millionth mini the  car owed its success to its  revolutionary concept  christopher says compacts were a mess in  the 1950s there were two stroke engines  and four stroke ones engines in the  front and back front and rear wheel  drive it was anything goes until the  mini came and revolutionized things  in the early 1950s the ideal economy car  still seemed a long way off  in france the situant ii cv brought  mobility to the masses with its  two-cylinder air-cooled front engine and  front-wheel drive  in germany the rear-wheel drive bmw 600  had a two-cylinder boxer engine in the  back its front doubled as the front door  since 1945 volkswagen had producing its  robust beetle powered by a four-cylinder  boxer engine in the trunk  and renault gave its 4cv a water-cooled  four-cylinder inline engine also mounted  at the back  then along came the mini a traversely  mounted front engine with four cylinders  and front-wheel drive squeezing the  technology into a small space left lots  of room for passengers  our tester says the key was lightweight  construction with the mini everything  really is mini even in here mini comfort  many standard features and mini seats  there's not even a door handle just a  pole here to open it but all this  slashes the car's weight the mini weighs  barely 600 kilos so christoph says its  34 horsepower engine has more than  enough pep  austin claimed that on average the mini  accelerated 30 percent faster and used  10 percent less fuel than its  competitors it could go from 0 to 130  seconds and reach a top speed of 120  kilometers an hour but those figures  alone hardly explain how the mini became  the stuff of legend  christoph says the car has that famous  go-kart feel this is due to its small  wheels and low center of gravity as well  as the hydroelastic suspension which  keeps it from veering to the side on  corners so he says it's lots of fun  driving the mini on twisty roads  in the city too this agile little car  can maneuver through the smallest of  spaces although it's just three meters  in length the mini can seat four adults  for such a small car it's very spacious  inside kristoff says  but then he says the mini was always a  giant among small cars  everything possible was done to save  room for the interior  the four-cylinder engine is mounted  transversely with the transmission in  the sump  and the radiators at the left  but what christoph likes best is the  trunk  he says that while it doesn't offer much  room for luggage you can also put your  suitcase here on the tailgate and to  make sure you can still see it from  behind that license plate folds out too  christoph says this has been really well  thought out something he can't say about  all british-built cars  the mini is chock full of clever ideas  the door hinges and standing seams are  located on the outside thereby  increasing interior space and cutting  production costs  the car's tiny 10 inch wheels and  correspondingly small wheel arches are  space savers too  the sliding windows in the absence of a  regular dashboard also mean more room  for passengers  what they sacrifice and features is made  up for by the increased cargo space an  ample leg and head room  it's four cylinder engine with 848  centimeters of cubic capacity generates  25 kilowatts of power that's enough to  tool around the city or enjoy leisurely  drives in the country  almost 5.4 million minis have been  produced making it the top selling  british car of all time  the car has fans around the globe and as  love is blind they're willing to  overlook the mini's weaknesses  christoph says he has a few complaints  like the heating and ventilation which  don't work at all he says you really  have to stomp on the brakes and even  then they're not too responsive the air  box isn't terribly well synchronized  either and then there's the price the  mini cost over 5 000 deutsche marks in  germany over a thousand more than a vw  beetle  today a standard austin mini in good  condition cost between six and twelve  thousand euros but expect a fork over  three times that much for a sporty  cooper version but to experience the  fascination of the mini an entry-level  model will do  christoph says legend has it that  engineer alec izagonis came up with the  idea during dinner and sketched out the  design for the mini on the tablecloth  and napkins what he created was  essentially the blueprint for all modern  compacts and remains the standard to  this day so he says the mini is  definitely a milestone in automotive  history  the original mini was produced for 41  years from 1959 to the year 2000 and  little wonder this forerunner of the  modern compact is a truly timeless car
 

